WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.140
The fourth episode of Star Trek: Picard, titled
"Absolute Candor," finally sees the former

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:10.430
captain take to the stars, building up his
ragtag crew as he searches for answers about

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:11.840
Data's twin daughters.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.331
From notable historical figures to nods to
previous Star Trek episodes, here are the

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:17.700
Easter eggs you missed.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:23.960
Prior to the synthetic attack on Mars 14 years
earlier, Picard was visiting an order of Romulan

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:30.260
assassin nuns called the Qowat Milat on the
planet Vashti . The Qowat Milat are also caring

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:35.480
for a young boy named Elnor, a refugee Picard
entrusted to the order until a more suitable

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:36.860
home can be found for him.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:42.420
Despite Picard's notorious distaste for children,
he has a soft spot for Elnor, and brings the

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.380
boy a copy of Alexandre Dumas' The Three Musketeers.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.880
Picard reads the book aloud to Elnor, and
the two of them enthusiastically enact some

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.180
of its more energetic fencing scenes before
Picard receives the tragic news about Mars.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:00.360
While The Three Musketeers is a popular tale
that has been referenced numerous times in

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:05.299
Star Trek history, the book plays a significant
role in the Next Generation episode "Hollow

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:09.960
Pursuits," in a which a crewmember of the
Enterprise creates a holodeck program in which

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.940
Picard, Data, and Geordi play the Musketeers,
with Riker joining later on as a diminutive

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:16.800
D'Artagnan.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.440
In "Hollow Pursuits," the Musketeer version
of Picard challenges the real-life Riker to

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.300
a duel.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:27.440
But when Riker refuses, saying he has no sword
and doesn't want to fight, the virtual Picard

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:28.020
taunts him.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.740
"Do I detect a streak of yellow along the
good fellow's back?"

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:41.080
Later on in "Absolute Candor," Picard will
be challenged to a duel, throw down his sword,

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.280
and refuse to fight.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:48.200
Early on in "Absolute Candor," Dr. Agnes Jurati
wanders onto the bridge, desperate for some

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.000
conversation to take her mind off of the boredom
and monotony of space travel.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.940
When Rios seems reluctant to talk, Jurati
runs through a list of the things she's already

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.990
done to occupy her time, complaining that
she was going to watch a holo, but all he

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.900
has on board is Klingon opera.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:07.880
While we're definitely curious what the story
is behind Rios' Klingon opera collection,

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.660
the unpleasantness of Klingon opera is a long-running
joke in Star Trek.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:16.440
Characters from multiple Star Trek iterations
have repeatedly likened listening to Klingon

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.680
opera to a form of torture.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.700
"Here you were, ready to have a nice night
watching the karo-net match and you wind up

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.720
spending an agonizing evening listening to
Klingon opera."

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:33.299
However, on both Next Generation and Deep
Space Nine, Worf had a great love for Klingon

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.260
opera, and prided himself on his extensive
Klingon opera collection.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.940
"You're afraid that I might lose your precious
operas?"

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:44.500
"Yes."

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.940
Worf was even known to occasionally break
out in song himself if he thought no one was

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:50.580
listening.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:56.500
Rios' Klingon opera holos might indicate that
he and Worf share a similar taste in music,

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:01.480
or possibly that sometime before Rios' crew
consisted entirely of holographic versions

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:06.520
of himself, the La Sirena may have been home
to yet another Klingon music aficionado.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:12.800
When Narek asks Soji how she speaks such excellent
Romulan, she says that she learned it from

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.880
a friend of her father, who was a professor.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.100
Narek observes that she must have learned
it prior to May of 2396, when her records

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.700
say that she shipped out to the Beta Quadrant
aboard the Ellison.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.220
Most of the ships mentioned on Star Trek have
some sort of deeper significance, and the

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:29.340
Ellison is no exception.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.620
The ship is likely named for Harlan Ellison,
an influential and prolific writer who passed

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.080
away in 2018.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.339
One of Ellison's best known works was the
Star Trek episode "The City on the Edge of

00:03:40.339 --> 00:03:44.629
Forever," which is widely considered one of
the original series' finest episodes, although

00:03:44.629 --> 00:03:48.930
Ellison famously insisted that the episode
that aired was drastically different than

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:52.120
the one he'd written, and that he did not
approve of the changes.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:58.359
"I felt that they had mucked it up badly,
and it took I think six or seven years before

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.500
Gene Roddenberry and I even spoke to each
other again."

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:07.340
In the episode, McCoy accidentally travels
back in time to 1930s New York and inadvertently

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.360
alters history, and Kirk and Spock then follow
him in order to restore the timeline.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:17.420
Ellison won several awards for his writing
on the episode, including a Hugo Award, and

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.139
"The City on the Edge of Forever" is still
cited as one of the best Star Trek episodes

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:22.140
of all time.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:28.340
When Picard beams down to the Romulan relocation
settlement on the planet Vashti in "Absolute

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:32.340
Candor," he's unprepared for the frosty welcome
he receives from its inhabitants.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:37.720
One man confronts Picard, saying he recalled
when Picard came to Romulus with "great big

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.240
Wallenberg-class transports" to help relocate
the Romulan people to safety.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.419
Wallenberg-class transports are a new type
of ship in the Star Trek universe, and are

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:53.430
likely named for Raoul Wallenberg, a Swedish
diplomat credited with saving tens of thousands

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:57.300
of Jewish people in Nazi-occupied Hungary
during World War II.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.800
It's an apt name for transports designed to
carry people to safety in the face of extinction,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.220
although also a tragic one, given the Federation's
abandoning of their rescue efforts shortly

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:07.080
thereafter.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:13.020
Wallenberg's story was turned into the television
film Wallenberg: A Hero's Story in 1985, which

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:17.710
was overseen by Rick Berman, who served as
executive producer for numerous Star Trek

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:23.280
series and films, including The Next Generation,
Deep Space Nine, Voyager, and Enterprise.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:29.440
In "Absolute Candor," the specific Wallenberg-class
ship that the former Romulan senator says

00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:33.690
he boarded during the evacuation of Romulus
was named the Nightingale.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:38.810
Of course, this ship name is an obvious reference
to Florence Nightingale, the 19th century

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.620
woman who is commonly credited as being the
founder of modern nursing.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:47.919
Nightingale was also the name of an episode
of Star Trek: Voyager, in which Ensign Harry

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.840
Kim takes command of a Kraylor medical transport,
which he renames the Nightingale.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:54.680
"Nightingale.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.100
The name of someone from my homeworld.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.660
She was famous for treating wounded soldiers
on the battlefield."

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:05.860
Kim believes he is helping the Kraylor transport
vaccines to their homeworld, but eventually

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:10.610
finds out that their actual mission is to
transport a group of Kraylor scientists and

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:15.200
their prototype of a cloaking device to their
planet, in violation of the Prime Directive.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.560
In "Absolute Candor," the Romulans are angry
at Picard, believing that he willfully deceived

00:06:20.569 --> 00:06:25.659
and took advantage of the Romulan people during
a time when they were most vulnerable, while

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:30.060
in the "Nightingale" episode of Voyager, it
is Kim, representing the Federation, who was

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.060
deceived.

